# Container build files MUST keep LF line endings.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites the working
# tree to CRLF on checkout. That silently breaks Linux container builds:
#
#   * a CRLF shell script dies inside the container with
#     "/docker-entrypoint.d/10-apibaseurl.sh: no such file or directory" or
#     "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" — the \r becomes part of the interpreter path;
#   * a CRLF Dockerfile breaks backslash line-continuations;
#   * a CRLF .env leaves a trailing \r inside every value docker compose reads —
#     including the treasury mnemonic, which would then simply be wrong.
#
# The failure only appears on a FRESH CLONE, never for whoever authored the file, which is
# what makes it worth pinning explicitly. Deliberately scoped to container files: no
# repo-wide EOL policy is imposed here, so no existing source file is renormalised.

*.sh            text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml   text eol=lf
docker-compose.*.yml text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
